    "# Lifestyle and Preventive Recommendations for Heart Health ## Diet A heart-healthy diet emphasizes vegetables, fruits, whole grains, lean protein, and healthy fats (such as those found in olive oil, nuts, and fish), while limiting saturated fat, added sugar, and sodium. The Mediterranean and DASH dietary patterns are commonly recommended for reducing cardiovascular risk. ## Physical Activity Adults are generally encouraged to aim for at least 150 minutes per week of moderate-intensity aerobic activity (such as brisk walking), combined with muscle-strengthening activity on two or more days per week, subject to individual medical clearance. ## Smoking Cessation Quitting smoking is one of the most effective single steps a person can take to reduce cardiovascular risk. Risk begins to decline within weeks of quitting and continues to fall over subsequent years. ## Weight Management Maintaining a healthy body weight through diet and exercise reduces strain on the heart and improves blood pressure, cholesterol, and blood sugar control. ## Blood Pressure and Cholesterol Monitoring Regular monitoring allows early detection of hypertension or unfavorable lipid levels, enabling timely lifestyle changes or medical treatment before complications develop. ## Diabetes Management For individuals with diabetes, maintaining blood sugar within target ranges through diet, exercise, and medication as prescribed helps protect blood vessels and reduce cardiovascular complications. ## Stress Management and Sleep Chronic stress and poor sleep are associated with elevated blood pressure and unhealthy coping behaviors. Adequate sleep and stress-reduction practices (such as relaxation techniques or regular physical activity) support overall cardiovascular health. ## Regular Checkups Routine checkups allow healthcare providers to assess risk factors, order appropriate screening tests, and adjust prevention or treatment plans over time.",
    "# Heart Disease Risk Factors ## Age and Sex Cardiovascular risk increases with age. Men generally face elevated risk earlier in life, while risk in women rises notably after menopause due to hormonal changes affecting cholesterol and blood vessel health. ## Smoking Smoking damages blood vessel walls, raises blood pressure, reduces oxygen in the blood, and significantly increases the likelihood of plaque buildup in the arteries (atherosclerosis). Smokers have roughly two to four times the risk of developing coronary heart disease compared to non-smokers. ## Diabetes High blood sugar over time damages blood vessels and the nerves that control the heart. Adults with diabetes are at substantially higher risk of heart disease and tend to develop it at a younger age than people without diabetes. ## Hypertension (High Blood Pressure) Persistently high blood pressure forces the heart to work harder, leading to thickening of the heart muscle and stiffening of the arteries. It is one of the leading contributors to heart attack and stroke risk. ## High Cholesterol Elevated LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol contributes to fatty deposits in artery walls, narrowing them and restricting blood flow. Low HDL (\"good\") cholesterol reduces the body's ability to clear excess cholesterol. ## Obesity Excess body weight is associated with higher blood pressure, unfavorable cholesterol levels, and increased risk of type 2 diabetes \u2014 all of which compound cardiovascular risk. ## Family History A family history of early heart disease (in a father or brother before age 55, or a mother or sister before age 65) raises individual risk, suggesting a genetic or shared-environment component. ## Physical Inactivity Lack of regular physical activity contributes to obesity, high blood pressure, poor cholesterol profiles, and reduced cardiovascular fitness, all of which elevate heart disease risk. ## Chest Pain (Angina) Chest pain or discomfort, especially during exertion, can signal that the heart muscle is not getting enough oxygen-rich blood, often due to narrowed coronary arteries. It should always be evaluated by a clinician.",
    "# When to Seek Medical Care ## Warning Signs of a Heart Attack Warning signs can include chest pain or pressure, discomfort spreading to the arms, back, neck, jaw, or stomach, shortness of breath, cold sweat, nausea, and lightheadedness. Symptoms can differ between individuals, and some people, particularly women, may experience less typical symptoms such as fatigue or indigestion-like discomfort. ## Emergency Response Any suspected heart attack symptoms should be treated as a medical emergency. Seeking immediate emergency care is critical, as prompt treatment significantly improves outcomes. ## Routine Risk Assessment Individuals with multiple risk factors (such as smoking, diabetes, hypertension, high cholesterol, obesity, family history, or physical inactivity) are encouraged to discuss personalized screening and prevention plans with a healthcare provider, even in the absence of symptoms. ## Role of AI-Based Risk Tools AI-based risk prediction tools can help highlight patterns and risk factors for further discussion with a clinician, but they are not a substitute for professional medical evaluation, diagnostic testing, or clinical judgment."
  ],
